DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       If all you want to do is convert PGM to PPM, keeping the same gray pix‐
       els,
         you may not need to.  All Netpbm programs that expect PPM input also
         recognize PGM.  And if you must have a PPM file, use ppppmmttooppppmm
         instead.  It is more efficient and easier to use.

       ppggmmttooppppmm reads a PGM as input and produces a PPM file as output with  a
       specific color assigned to each gray value in the input.

       You  can  specify  the  color in the output to which black in the input
       maps,
         and the color to which white maps.  All the gray  values  in  between
       map
         linearly  (across  a  three  dimensional space) to colors between the
       black and
         white colors you specify.

       Use the --bbllaacckk and --wwhhiittee options for this.  For historical
         reasons, you can alternatively use a non-option argument  to  specify
       the
         colors.  If you do that, ppggmmttooppppmm interprets the color argument
         like this: if the argument takes the form _b_l_a_c_k-_w_h_i_t_e,
         it has the effect of --bbllaacckk==_b_l_a_c_k --wwhhiittee==_w_h_i_t_e
         If  instead  there is no hyphen in the color argument, it has the ef‐
       fect of
         --wwhhiittee==_c_o_l_o_r___a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t.

       Because of the historical syntax, it is not possible to let both
         --bbllaacckk and --wwhhiittee default (but you shouldn't want to --
         see below for advice on making such a null conversion).


       You can alternatively specify an entire colormap with the --mmaapp option.


       A more direct way to specify a particular color to replace each partic‐
       ular  gray  level is to use ppaammllooookkuupp.  You make an index file that ex‐
       plicitly associates a color with each possible gray level.



OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common  Options  ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppggmmttooppppmm recognizes the
       following command line options:




       --bbllaacckk==_c_o_l_o_r_s_p_e_c
              The program maps black pixels in the input to this color in  the
              output.  The default is black.

              Specify  the  color (_c_o_l_o_r) as described for the argument of the
              ppnnmm__ppaarrsseeccoolloorr(())                 library                 routine
              ⟨libnetpbm_image.html#colorname⟩ .

              You cannot specify this together with --mmaapp.

              This  option  was  new  in Netpbm 10.97 (December 2021).  Before
              that,
                use the color argument.


       --wwhhiittee==_c_o_l_o_r_s_p_e_c
              The program maps white pixels in the input to this color in  the
              output.  The default is white.

              Specify  the  color (_c_o_l_o_r) as described for the argument of the
              ppnnmm__ppaarrsseeccoolloorr(())                 library                 routine
              ⟨libnetpbm_image.html#colorname⟩ .

              You cannot specify this together with --mmaapp.

              This  option  was  new  in Netpbm 10.97 (December 2021).  Before
              that,
                use the color argument.


       --mmaapp==_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e
              This option specifies a complete mapping of gray values  in  the
              input to
                  color  values  in the output.  The map file (named _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e)
              is just
                  a ppppmm file; it can be any shape, all  that  matters  is  the
              colors in
                  it  and  their  order.  In this case, black gets mapped into
              the first color
                  in the map file, and white gets mapped to the last and  gray
              values in
                  between  are  mapped linearly onto the sequence of colors in
              between.  The
                  maxval of the output image is the maxval of the map image.





NNOOTTEE -- MMAAXXVVAALL
       When you don't use --mmaapp, the "maxval," or depth, of the output image is
       the same as that of the input image.  The maxval affects the color res‐
       olution, which may cause quantization errors you  don't  anticipate  in
       your output.  For example, you have a simple black and white image as a
       PGM with maxval 1.  Run this image through ppggmmttooppppmm 00ff//0000//0000 to try  to
       make the image black and faint red.  Because the output image will also
       have maxval 1, there is no such thing as faint red.  It has to  be  ei‐
       ther  full-on red or black.  ppggmmttooppppmm rounds the color 0f/00/00 down to
       black, and you get an output image that is nothing but black.

       The fix is easy: Pass the input through ppaammddeepptthh on the way  into  ppggmm‐‐
       ttooppppmm  to increase its depth to something that would give you the reso‐
       lution you need to get your desired color.  In this case,  ppaammddeepptthh  1166
       would  do  it.  Or spare yourself the unnecessary thinking and just say
       ppaammddeepptthh 225555.

       PBM input is a special case.  While  you  might  think  this  would  be
       equivalent to a PGM with maxval 1 since only two gray levels are neces‐
       sary to represent a PBM image, ppggmmttooppppmm, like all Netpbm  programs,  in
       fact treats it as a maxval of 255.


